  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nComplicated short-term forecast setup this evening and tonight, with \nmesoscale factors likely determining the extent of the thunderstorm \nand severe weather threat. Weak convection is currently still \nongoing across portions of northern and eastern KS, driven by some \nweak 700 mb WAA. As we head into the late afternoon and evening \nhours, watching two areas closely for additional and stronger \nconvection. The first area is along the composite frontal and \noutflow boundary across southern portions of the forecast area \n(along/south of a line from Morris to Coffey Counties). Convection \nalong this boundary is more conditional, and may stay south of the \narea, but the environment is favorable for a more discrete \nsupercell. So any storm that does develop could pose a threat for \ndamaging winds 60-70 mph and very large hail up to 2 inches \ndiameter. Farther north towards the Nebraska border, convection \nshould continue to redevelop along the 850 mb front. Here, storms \nwill remain elevated north of the surface boundary. However shear \nand instability should still be enough for some isolated damaging \nwinds or large hail to around 1 inch. As isentropic ascent continues \novernight, storms will likely become more numerous north of the \nboundary. This should result in a weakening trend but will have to \nwatch for any pockets of training convection. Can't rule out some \nlocally heavy rainfall totals of 2-4\" if this occurs.\n\nThe overnight convection should reinforce the boundary for Sunday, \nthough there is still some uncertainty in exactly how far south it \nends up. But particularly if clouds and showers linger to the north, \nwe could see high temperatures range from the low 70s across far \nnortheast KS to the upper 80s in southern Coffey and Anderson \nCounties. Confidence remains high that the boundary will lift north \nof the area Monday as a warm front. Hot and humid conditions will be \nin place as a result, with temperatures climbing into the 90s with \nheat indices near or a bit above 100. \n\nMonday night into Tuesday and Wednesday, an upper low moving across \nthe Northern Plains will push a cold front slowly east across the \narea. Should see several rounds of showers and storms along and \nbehind this front through Tuesday evening. This will keep \ntemperatures behind the front much cooler, in the 70s and even 60s. \nEast of the front, temperatures will warm into the 80s and 90s. \nStill a fair amount of uncertainty in how far east the front will \nmake it before stalling on Wednesday, so there remains a large \nspread in forecast temperatures Tuesday into Wednesday and even \nlater on in the week. Regardless, with multiple rounds of rain \npossible along the front, there is the potential for several inches \nof rain over the week, especially across northwestern portions of \nthe forecast area. The very dry last 40 days should limit flooding \npotential, especially spread out over several days, but it's at \nleast something to watch.",
